## Practical Workflows for Human teams

In healthcare, working on stringent deadlines, regulations, restrictions and specificities makes innovation a challenge - technologies are often antiquated,
bureaucracy runs amok in the industry, and the industry, as a whole is unarguably bloated with institutional challenges. This makes it particularly difficult
to automate things, when regulatory actions and frameworks are constantly changing. As a company that relies on accurate data to infer actionable
and important insight on patient demographics, simply abstracting things often gets in the way of getting things done.

There lies the fine balance of WHAT needs to be done vs HOW it should be done. 

HOW do we design systems that are robust enough to adjust to the constant changes, whether it be regulatory guidelines (e.g. mammograms need to be done for women over 45 years old as of 2025, or colonoscopies for 45 year olds as of 2025)?
In a small company or startup that needs to move fast, this is particularly challenging, as the need to get things done often supersedes the need to abstract things away.

The human touch, inevitably, is the most important aspect of running any patient facing healthcare company. At wellPORTAL, we run a small call center that handles
patient needs daily, allows agents to sort and manage tasks, make appointments, handle patient needs, complete with time and productivity tracking. From our clients, who
demand specific, minute by minute changes to how they want their health plans to be administered, over engineering things prevents us from being flexible, which is our primary advantage.

For our data needs, we tag data at the source. Patient data in healthcare arrives from everywhere — EHRs, claims feeds, lab systems, eligibility files — each with different lag, reliability, and quirks.
One of the easiest ways to erode trust in a platform is to treat all of it as equivalent. We track source, timestamp, and confidence through to the insight level. 
When a care gap fires, we want to know exactly what data triggered it. Clinical teams act on these signals; they need to be able to interrogate them.
